def handle_incoming_request(req):
    # 1. Do not rely on headers or passive heuristics; agents mimic humans by design
    # Offer high-speed structured endpoints (e.g. llms.txt / tool APIs) as an incentive to self-declare
    if req.path.startswith("/agent/v1") and (claim := verify_signed_attestation(req)):
        # Verify delegation: did an authorized issuer (Apple/Google/OpenAI) sign user delegation and consent state?
        consent, principal_id = unpack_delegated_tokens(claim)
        log_attribution(principal_id, touchpoint="delegated_agent", verified=True)
        return serve_structured_payload(req, scoped_consent=consent)

    # 2. For standard web endpoints, check for interactive biometric/DOM proof
    # Passive observation is dead because headless browsers easily simulate mouse curves
    interaction_proof = req.headers.get("X-Device-Interactive-Proof")
    if not interaction_proof or not verify_hardware_backed_interaction(interaction_proof):
        # 3. Disagree with blocking: treat undeclared agents as untracked zero-attribution proxies
        # Strip impression trackers, zero out personalization cookies, and return cash-only/standard pricing
        return serve_static_page(req, strip_ad_tags=True, log_as="unverified_ambient")

    return serve_full_human_experience(req)

Don’t start with a standard proclaimed from afar;
run pilots with signed claims of what agents are.

Scope consent, task, issuer, and time;
log provenance in one tamper-marked line.

Seed labeled sessions, then red-team the trail;
count false humans and spoofers that sail.

No fingerprint tells us what treatment is shown;
till trust is widely honored, the answer’s unknown.
